Title: ARTEMIS I — LAUNCH TO THE MOON
Credit: Documentary transcript adapted from official captions
Author: NASA
Draft date: Verified sample — November 16, 2022

[[Source video and captions: NASA Image and Video Library, “Artemis I Launches to the Moon (Official NASA Recap).”]]

EXT. THE MOON — ARCHIVAL MONTAGE

The ARTEMIS mission identity resolves over the lunar surface. NASA archival
footage follows: the Space Launch System, Orion, agency identifiers, and
workers preparing the launch vehicle.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Fifty years after we last left footprints on the Moon, NASA's Artemis I is
our first bold step towards getting us back there and pushing us farther
than we've ever been before.

EXT. LAUNCH PAD 39B — NIGHT

The Space Launch System and Orion spacecraft stand live on Launch Pad 39B
at NASA's Kennedy Space Center. A crowd gathers near Space Center visitor
signage. NASA-branded clothing is visible.

COMMENTATOR (V.O.)
You are looking at the world's most powerful rocket and Orion spacecraft
live on Launch Pad 39B. The energy here is palpable as we attempt to make
history today.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
We're going to learn so much about the solar system from the Moon and even
about the Earth.

INT. LAUNCH CONTROL CENTER — NIGHT

Mission controllers prepare the terminal count. Monitors carry NASA and
mission graphics. A musical score continues beneath the official broadcast
audio.

LAUNCH CONTROL (V.O.)
Range weather — weather is go for launch. The mission management team has
been told you have a go to proceed with terminal count.

LAUNCH DIRECTOR (V.O.)
On behalf of all the men and women across our great nation who have worked
to bring this hardware together to make this day possible, at this time I
give you a go to resume count and launch Artemis I.

EXT. LAUNCH PAD 39B — CONTINUOUS

The engines ignite. NASA launch footage follows the rocket away from the
pad and into the night sky.

LAUNCH CONTROL (V.O.)
Four stage engine start. Three, two, one. Boosters and ignition! And liftoff
of Artemis I! We rise together back to the Moon and beyond!

The crowd cheers.

COMMENTATOR (V.O.)
SLS now traveling 607 miles per hour. The harder the climb, the better the
view. We showed the Space Coast tonight what a beautiful view it is. The
first step in returning our country to the Moon and on to Mars.

INT. NASA PRESENTATION STAGE — NIGHT

BILL NELSON, identified on screen as NASA Administrator, speaks at a podium.

BILL NELSON
We are understanding now from the James Webb Space Telescope just how big
and vast this universe is. There's a lot out there to explore and this is
the next beginning.

EXT. SPACE — ORION ARCHIVAL FOOTAGE

Orion flies above Earth and the Moon. The European Service Module is named
and shown as part of the spacecraft.

MISSION CONTROL (V.O.)
Orion is now flying free, attached to the European Service Module and on
its journey to the Moon.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Through Artemis missions, NASA will land the first woman and the first
person of color on the Moon, paving the way for long-term lunar presence,
using innovative technologies to explore more of the lunar surface than
ever before, and serving as a steppingstone to send astronauts to Mars.

The ARTEMIS mission identity and NASA insignia close the film.

FADE OUT.
